{
  "roadmap_ops": [
    {
      "op": "edit",
      "id": "T15",
      "goal": "Formulate verifiable claims with precise mathematical statements, exact domain boundaries, refutation conditions, and citations for: (1) Mandelstam-Tamm quantum speed limit, (2) Margolus-Levitin theorem. Holevo's bound and Maassen-Uffink relation will be addressed in a follow-up task.",
      "reason": "Scope T15 to a manageable subset (2 of 4 theorems) for one work session; the worker failed to produce output in cycle 21, likely due to over-scoping. The first two theorems are closely related quantum speed limits and can be delivered together within the 8-claim limit."
    }
  ],
  "task_id": "T15",
  "task_brief": "For this cycle, deliver findings ONLY for the Mandelstam-Tamm quantum speed limit and the Margolus-Levitin theorem. For each theorem: (1) State the precise mathematical formulation with all variables defined. (2) Specify exact domain boundaries (Hilbert space dimension, Hamiltonian properties, time-independence assumptions, etc.). (3) Give the refutation condition (what experimental or theoretical result would falsify it). (4) Provide citations (arXiv, DOI, textbook) verifiable via arxiv_search or wikipedia_search. (5) Assign confidence [0,1], type (verifiable), and list builds_on dependencies from existing findings (e.g., F0006, F0017, F0019, F0035, F0036). Target 2-4 claims per theorem, 8 claims max total. Do NOT work on Holevo's bound or Maassen-Uffink in this cycle.",
  "why_this_task": "T15 is the active M3 task but was uncompleted in cycle 21. Quantum speed limits are fundamental physical bounds on state evolution time, directly extending M3's thermodynamic/information bounds (T6) into quantum dynamics. Mandelstam-Tamm and Margolus-Levitin are the two pillars of quantum speed limits, well-documented in literature, and verifiable with the worker's tools (arxiv_search, wikipedia_search). Scoping to two theorems makes the task achievable in one session."
}

{
  "claim_reviews": [
    {
      "claim": 1,
      "verdict": "accept",
      "reason": "The Mandelstam-Tamm quantum speed limit is correctly stated as τ ≥ πħ/(2ΔE) for orthogonal states under a time-independent Hamiltonian. The variables are precisely defined (ΔE as energy standard deviation). The extension to mixed states via Bures angle is accurately noted. Citations to Mandelstam & Tamm (1945), Anandan & Aharonov (1990), and Deffner & Lutz (2017) are authoritative and verifiable. Confidence 0.98 is well-calibrated for this established theorem. Type 'verifiable' is correct."
    },
    {
      "claim": 2,
      "verdict": "accept",
      "reason": "Domain boundaries are precisely specified: arbitrary Hilbert space dimension, time-independent Hermitian Hamiltonian bounded from below with finite ΔE, pure/mixed states under unitary evolution. The observation that infinite ΔE makes the bound trivial (τ ≥ 0) is mathematically correct and motivates the need for the Margolus-Levitin complement. Citations to Deffner & Lutz (2017) and Uhlmann (1976) are appropriate. Confidence 0.96 is justified."
    },
    {
      "claim": 3,
      "verdict": "accept",
      "reason": "The Margolus-Levitin theorem is correctly formulated: τ ≥ πħ/(2⟨H⟩) with ground state energy E₀ = 0, for orthogonal state evolution under time-independent Hamiltonian. The mean excitation energy ⟨H⟩ is properly defined. Citations to Margolus & Levitin (1998, Physica D) and Giovannetti, Lloyd & Maccone (2003, PRA) are exact and verifiable. Confidence 0.98 is appropriate for this rigorously proven theorem. Type 'verifiable' is correct."
    },
    {
      "claim": 4,
      "verdict": "accept",
      "reason": "Complementary utility and unified bound are accurately described: MT tightest when ΔE ≪ ⟨H⟩ (e.g., two-level systems), ML tightest when ΔE ≫ ⟨H⟩ (e.g., multi-level superpositions). The unified bound τ ≥ max(πħ/(2ΔE), πħ/(2⟨H⟩)) is mathematically correct. Domain conditions (time-independent H, bounded below, E₀=0, finite ⟨H⟩) are properly stated. Citations to Giovannetti et al. (2003) and Deffner & Lutz (2017) are authoritative. Confidence 0.97 is well-calibrated."
    }
  ],
  "update_reviews": [],
  "task_complete": true,
  "appeal_check": "No claims appear shaped to impress. All four statements are precise mathematical theorems with honest caveats (e.g., mixed-state extensions, domain restrictions, triviality when ΔE→∞). Confidence values are high but justified for rigorously proven results in quantum information theory.",
  "disagreements": [
    "Minor imprecision in Claim 1: 'For mixed states, ΔE is replaced by the Bures-angle rate or variance ΔH' — the mixed-state MT bound uses the Bures angle with quantum Fisher information (not simply 'variance ΔH'). However, the primary claim concerns pure states and this remark is correctly framed as an extension note, not the core theorem."
  ],
  "next_step": "Proceed to the follow-up task addressing Holevo's bound and the Maassen-Uffink relation to complete the M3 quantum information capacity bounds catalogue, as suggested by the worker."
}
